{ id:2013,q:"A marketplace removes sellers with repeated complaints. What is the likely effect?",options:["It improves the seller pool by removing lower-quality sellers","It increases hidden-quality problems","It creates moral hazard in lending","It eliminates the need for all buyer caution"],a:0,tag:"reputation",type:"interpretation",objective:"LO19.5",feedback:"Removing poor sellers can reduce adverse selection by improving the quality of sellers in the marketplace."},
{ id:2014,q:"Verified reviews help buyers decide whether to trust a seller. What information problem are they mainly reducing?",options:["Adverse selection from hidden seller quality","Shirking by employees after hiring","Transfer pricing conflict","Budget slack"],a:0,tag:"reputation",type:"interpretation",objective:"LO19.5",feedback:"Reviews make hidden seller quality more visible before future buyers purchase."},

// --- TIMING / DISTINCTION CHECKS ---
{ id:2015,q:"The Underwriter sets one final trap: 'Before or after?' A risky driver buys insurance because they know they are likely to crash. What is this?",options:["Adverse selection","Moral hazard","Transfer pricing","Coordination failure"],a:0,tag:"adverse_selection",type:"trap",objective:"LO20.1",feedback:"This is adverse selection because the hidden risk exists before the insurance contract."},
{ id:2016,q:"The Underwriter narrows his eyes: 'Before or after?' A driver becomes more reckless after buying full insurance. What is this?",options:["Moral hazard","Adverse selection","Screening","Signaling"],a:0,tag:"moral_hazard",type:"trap",objective:"LO20.1",feedback:"This is moral hazard because the behavior changes after coverage begins."},
{ id:2017,q:"The Underwriter gives the final order: 'Name the cleanest distinction.' How do adverse selection and moral hazard differ?",options:["Adverse selection is hidden type before the deal; moral hazard is hidden action after the deal","Adverse selection is hidden action after the deal; moral hazard is hidden type before the deal","Both only happen in transfer pricing","Both only happen when firms are cost centers"],a:0,tag:"moral_hazard",type:"definition",objective:"LO20.1",feedback:"Timing is the key. Adverse selection is before the transaction; moral hazard is after it."}
